/**
 * Roles & command-permissions for plugin-based free Minecraft servers, backed
 * entirely by LuckPerms (bundled on every `kind:"plugins"` FREEMC_PRESETS
 * entry — see lib/free-minecraft-control.ts). No new DB schema: LuckPerms owns
 * all role/permission state itself, inside the container, in its own
 * storage — every read below is a LIVE query against that storage, nothing is
 * cached or mirrored into Postgres.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * WHY READS GO THROUGH FILES, NOT RCON TEXT — READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING IT
 * ============================================================================
 * The obvious design (mirroring lib/free-minecraft-control.ts's sendRcon +
 * text-parsing pattern used everywhere else) is to run `/lp listgroups`,
 * `/lp group X permission info`, `/lp group X listmembers`, etc. over RCON
 * and parse the text response, the same way getFreeServerPlayers parses
 * `list`'s output. That was the ORIGINAL plan here — it does not work, and
 * this was proven with a real container (`mc-rolestest` on freemc-host,
 * itzg/minecraft-server, Paper 26.1.2, LuckPerms v5.5.71), not assumed:
 *
 *   - `/lp creategroup vip`, `/lp group X permission set ...`, `/lp user X
 *     parent add ...` etc. ALL return a completely EMPTY RCON response —
 *     every time, regardless of storage backend (tested against both LP's
 *     default H2 storage and yaml flat-file storage) and regardless of how
 *     long the RCON connection is kept open afterward (tested with an
 *     interactive rcon-cli session held open 4s+ past the command — still
 *     nothing).
 *   - Crucially, the commands DO still execute — confirmed by reading
 *     LuckPerms' own storage back after each one (the group/user file exists,
 *     with exactly the right permissions/parents). So this is not a broken
 *     feature, it is a broken CONFIRMATION CHANNEL: LuckPerms dispatches its
 *     command handling through its own internal thread pool, and whatever it
 *     sends back to the CommandSender arrives after Bukkit's RCON dispatch
 *     has already returned an (empty) response and torn down the feedback
 *     collector for that invocation. Nothing ever reaches the RCON socket,
 *     and nothing is logged to console either — the reply is just discarded.
 *   - This is specific to LuckPerms' own `/lp ...` command family. Plain
 *     synchronous commands on the SAME container (vanilla `list`/`seed`,
 *     Paper's `/plugins`, Essentials' `/balance`+`/eco give` argument-error
 *     paths) all return real text over the identical RCON path — so this is
 *     not a general RCON/plugin problem, it is specific to LP's async command
 *     execution model.
 *
 * So: MUTATIONS still go over RCON (sendRcon from free-minecraft-control.ts —
 * fire and forget, no text to parse), but every READ in this file — listing
 * roles, their granted nodes, their members — parses LuckPerms' own on-disk
 * storage directly over SSH (`cat`), which is fast, reliable, and exactly
 * "live" (nothing cached). After a mutation, this file always reads the
 * relevant file straight back to report real success/failure to the caller,
 * rather than trusting RCON's silence.
 *
 * That in turn requires LuckPerms be configured with flat-file (`yaml`)
 * storage rather than its H2-database default — an H2 file isn't something
 * this app can parse without a JDBC driver — and, separately, with
 * `use-server-uuid-cache: true`. That second one is its own confirmed
 * finding: with LuckPerms' DEFAULT config (`use-server-uuid-cache: false`),
 * `/lp user <name> parent add <group>` for a player who has NEVER joined
 * silently does nothing at all — no user file is ever created. Flipping that
 * one config key fixed it immediately (verified: the resulting user file
 * appears, keyed by the standard offline-mode UUID — see offlineUuidFor
 * below, whose output was checked byte-for-byte against what LuckPerms
 * itself produced for the same username). Without this, "assign a role by
 * username without requiring the player to be online" — an explicit
 * requirement here — would only work for players who had already connected
 * at least once, which defeats the point.
 *
 * ensureLuckPermsReady() below applies both settings idempotently (a cheap
 * `cat` + regex check first; only sed+restarts the container the first time
 * a given container actually needs it). Note this can also need to re-fire
 * after a PRESET SWITCH: applyFreeMinecraftPreset (lib/actions-free-minecraft.ts,
 * off-limits to this feature) wipes `/data/plugins/*` on every preset change,
 * which deletes LuckPerms' whole config+storage along with it — so config
 * drifting back to defaults after a preset switch is expected, not a bug,
 * and this file re-detects and re-applies every time rather than assuming a
 * one-time fix sticks forever.
